cartridge "BAGDAD/K.MARTIN B.FILES/1984"

' BAGDAD
' A ColecoVision game based on "Bagdad" by Kevin Martin and Bryan Files,
' COMPUTE!'s Gazette November 1984 (Commodore 64 machine language started
' by SYS 2061, and a VIC-20 version; authors from LaunchBox's entry, the
' disk names none). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by
' Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' The game: you are the good genie on a magic rug. Bad genies on rugs fly
' over the city. Touch one from above and it is knocked off its rug into a
' bottle; touch one from below and you are the one knocked off. Collect
' every bottle before its genie gets free again: that clears the wave.
' Points only for bottles (250 each). Three genies (two in reserve), one
' more every 5000 points (at most 7 in reserve).
'
' From the original (orig/bagdad_64.dis, run in jsc64): the button flies
' up, letting go falls (the vertical speed steps by one each tick either
' way, capped), the stick pushes left/right and the rug keeps its speed in
' the air; the screen: two arcades left and right on two levels, one in
' the middle, a street; when two genies touch the higher one wins; the
' knocked genie's bottle drops to the ground; its time runs ($1525: 120
' ticks for genies 1-4, 90 for 5-6, 70 for 7, so the later colours open
' sooner), then it changes to a smoking bottle for 100 ticks and the genie
' flies out; the bad genies pick a new plan every 32 ticks (keep going,
' chase you, flee you, or coast) and now and then a height plan (get
' above you, or a height of their own); waves: wave n has (n + 3) / 2 - 1
' genies, at most 7 (white 1-4, then two more, the 7th from wave 13);
' new genies appear at five places (the middle arcade half the time, else
' one of the four side arcades), never on top of another; 250 points a
' bottle and none for anything else; three genies (two shown in reserve),
' one more every 5000 points up to 7 in reserve; after a loss you wait at
' the bottom middle, safe and blinking, until you move (or a short time
' passes); "WAVE n" and a jingle between waves; score and high score.
'
' Liberties: the sides are walls the rugs bounce off (the C64 wrapped
' round the screen edge, which a TV crops); rugs land on the arcades and
' the street and slow down there when the stick is let go (the C64
' bounced off every surface); touching within 3 pixels of the same height
' bumps both apart (the C64 gave you the tie); the bad genies can rise 8
' pixels higher than you (else hugging the top of the screen made you
' unbeatable) and they turn away when they are just below you; speeds are
' per 60 Hz frame and the bad genies start slower than you, speed up and
' go for you more often each wave, so a person can play (the C64 gave them
' your speed); a genie re-plans every 4 frames and looks at your height
' every 32 (a fair, human-like lag); bottle times in frames (300 for
' genies 1-4, 240 for 5-6, 180 for 7, plus 100 of smoke); a bottle can be
' caught 15 frames after it drops; colours: white, cyan and magenta genies
' (the C64's blue would vanish against this sky); a puff when you are
' knocked off; the title tune and the sounds are new; GAME OVER then the
' title.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): the button (either) flies up, the stick
' left/right steers. On the title, up/down choose and a button starts.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128;
' frame loop protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off after a
' wait, display off; music first in the sound table, the title tune played
' once; `set sprite count` always given; nothing flashes faster than 3 Hz.

